WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.380
Hi, I'm Nei Maldaner,

2
00:00:01.380 --> 00:00:03.060
and this is track five of the

3
00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:04.080
LOOP-R course.

4
00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:05.720
In the previous four tracks,

5
00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:07.080
you saw each part of the loop

6
00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:07.620
on its own:

7
00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:09.880
the worksheet, the tracking spreadsheet,

8
00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:12.080
the guardrails, and the decision card.

9
00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:14.460
Now we’ll see these parts working together,

10
00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:15.780
with real numbers.

11
00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:17.780
First, a sales loop at a beauty

12
00:00:17.780 --> 00:00:19.740
clinic, from start to finish,

13
00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:21.660
across four cycles.

14
00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:23.420
Then a support loop,

15
00:00:23.420 --> 00:00:25.820
where forgotten tickets lead to a better

16
00:00:25.820 --> 00:00:26.320
product.

17
00:00:26.120 --> 00:00:27.140
Finally,

18
00:00:27.140 --> 00:00:28.340
your

19
00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:30.060
loop, assembled:

20
00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:32.180
the worksheet with five answers,

21
00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:33.600
the first decision card,

22
00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:36.600
and a canvas with thirteen questions for

23
00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:37.600
any process.

24
00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:39.480
Each lesson has a practice.

25
00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:40.620
When I ask,

26
00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:42.360
pause the video and do it right

27
00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:42.860
then.

28
00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:44.820
This lesson’s case is the course’s reference

29
00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:45.960
example:

30
00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:48.660
a beauty clinic that sends proposals over

31
00:00:48.660 --> 00:00:49.620
WhatsApp.

32
00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:51.240
One honest note:

33
00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:53.240
all nine assistants ran for real,

34
00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:55.860
without edits, but the proposals and client

35
00:00:55.860 --> 00:00:57.320
replies are simulated.

36
00:00:57.320 --> 00:00:59.600
This case shows how the loop behaves;

37
00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:01.120
it is not a discovery.

38
00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:03.400
The owner, let’s call her Renata,

39
00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:05.600
answered the worksheet’s five questions.

40
00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:08.400
Goal: convert proposals into paid packages,

41
00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:09.720
from three to five percent.

42
00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:10.880
Evidence:

43
00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:13.580
a spreadsheet, one row per proposal,

44
00:01:13.580 --> 00:01:15.480
about fifty a week.

45
00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:17.560
What never changes on its own:

46
00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:18.880
discounts over ten percent,

47
00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:19.860
promises

48
00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:21.300
about results or timing,

49
00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:23.360
contacting anyone who opted out,

50
00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:25.060
or mentioning a competitor.

51
00:01:25.060 --> 00:01:27.420
A limit of fifty reais per weekly

52
00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:28.620
cycle.

53
00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:30.240
And Renata approves,

54
00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:31.880
with seven days to respond.

55
00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:34.460
The system returned a proposal that Renata

56
00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:36.340
only had to confirm:

57
00:01:36.340 --> 00:01:38.440
first test a signal metric,

58
00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:39.420
the response rate,

59
00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:40.900
from twenty to thirty percent,

60
00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:43.160
while keeping an eye on conversion.

61
00:01:43.160 --> 00:01:44.140
There’s a reason:

62
00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:44.660
the math.

63
00:01:44.660 --> 00:01:45.600
Proving

64
00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:46.900
three against

65
00:01:47.307 --> 00:01:50.060
five percent takes about fifteen hundred proposals

66
00:01:50.060 --> 00:01:50.820
per version.

67
00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:52.080
At fifty a week,

68
00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:53.780
that would take sixty weeks.

69
00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:57.107
Proving twenty against thirty takes about three

70
00:01:57.107 --> 00:01:57.607
hundred.

71
00:01:57.440 --> 00:01:59.220
It also came with three guardrails:

72
00:01:59.220 --> 00:02:01.140
average margin, complaints,

73
00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:02.170
and

74
00:02:02.170 --> 00:02:03.260
opt-out requests.

75
00:02:03.260 --> 00:02:04.360
From here on,

76
00:02:04.360 --> 00:02:06.700
read each cycle in four lines.

77
00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:08.920
Evidence, with a count.

78
00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:10.700
Ideas and the Guardian’s veto.

79
00:02:10.700 --> 00:02:11.800
Test

80
00:02:11.800 --> 00:02:13.260
and verdict, A versus B.

81
00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:14.980
Then decision and memory.

82
00:02:14.980 --> 00:02:15.920
Cycle one.

83
00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:18.980
The Observer read two hundred proposals.

84
00:02:18.980 --> 00:02:21.680
The Critic spotted the pain point:

85
00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:23.813
one hundred sixty-six of them,

86
00:02:23.813 --> 00:02:26.940
eighty-three percent, got no reply within forty-eight

87
00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:27.620
hours.

88
00:02:27.620 --> 00:02:29.860
The Critic also found a mismatch:

89
00:02:29.860 --> 00:02:32.720
the worksheet said three percent conversion,

90
00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:33.920
while

91
00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:36.045
the spreadsheet showed

92
00:02:36.045 --> 00:02:37.080
two point five.

93
00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:38.220
The

94
00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:40.520
worksheet records what someone believes;

95
00:02:40.520 --> 00:02:43.140
the spreadsheet records what happened.

96
00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:45.510
The Optimizer wrote three ideas:

97
00:02:45.510 --> 00:02:47.720
end with a forced-choice question,

98
00:02:47.720 --> 00:02:48.800
like

99
00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:51.020
would you rather start this week or

100
00:02:51.020 --> 00:02:53.740
next; limit the message to eighty words;

101
00:02:53.740 --> 00:02:56.740
and open by mentioning something specific from

102
00:02:56.740 --> 00:02:57.820
that client’s review.

103
00:02:57.820 --> 00:02:59.620
The Guardian vetoed

104
00:02:59.620 --> 00:03:01.460
the first idea before any test.

105
00:03:01.460 --> 00:03:03.940
It assumes the client will buy and

106
00:03:03.940 --> 00:03:05.520
sets a deadline.

107
00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:06.800
A veto is final,

108
00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:09.500
and the Guardian doesn’t suggest an alternative.

109
00:03:09.500 --> 00:03:11.100
Then came the calculation.

110
00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:13.650
The Optimizer expected to raise replies from

111
00:03:13.650 --> 00:03:15.260
seventeen to twenty-two percent.

112
00:03:15.260 --> 00:03:18.060
The Experimenter calculated that proving a difference

113
00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:20.780
that size would take forty weeks.

114
00:03:20.780 --> 00:03:22.920
Renata reset the target to match the

115
00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:24.080
worksheet’s thirty percent.

116
00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:26.580
The test dropped to one hundred sixty-six

117
00:03:26.580 --> 00:03:27.860
proposals per version,

118
00:03:27.860 --> 00:03:29.400
or seven weeks.

119
00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:31.420
Since only one test runs at a

120
00:03:31.420 --> 00:03:33.700
time, the eighty-word idea went in,

121
00:03:33.700 --> 00:03:36.000
while the specific opening waited in the

122
00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:36.500
queue.

123
00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:38.100
If you’re a real estate agent,

124
00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:40.020
the same situation applies to you.

125
00:03:40.020 --> 00:03:41.860
Asking whether a client wants to visit

126
00:03:41.860 --> 00:03:43.880
on Saturday or Sunday would get the

127
00:03:43.880 --> 00:03:45.520
same veto from the Guardian.

128
00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:48.240
And replies within twenty-four hours are your

129
00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:50.240
signal metric, not the sale.

130
00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:51.347
Cycle

131
00:03:51.347 --> 00:03:51.893
two.

132
00:03:51.893 --> 00:03:53.280
Twelve weeks later,

133
00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:55.760
there were three hundred proposals on each

134
00:03:55.760 --> 00:03:56.260
side.

135
00:03:56.060 --> 00:03:58.640
Version A, the usual script:

136
00:03:58.640 --> 00:04:01.180
a nineteen point three percent response rate.

137
00:04:01.180 --> 00:04:04.280
Version B, with up to eighty words:

138
00:04:04.280 --> 00:04:05.800
twenty-nine point seven percent.

139
00:04:05.800 --> 00:04:08.480
The chance this was luck:

140
00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:09.720
three in a thousand.

141
00:04:09.720 --> 00:04:12.120
The Evaluator did not promote it.

142
00:04:12.120 --> 00:04:14.660
Complaints: six for B and one for

143
00:04:14.660 --> 00:04:17.460
A, while the worksheet’s tolerance was zero.

144
00:04:17.460 --> 00:04:19.280
Verdict: A stays.

145
00:04:19.280 --> 00:04:20.960
The rule was applied as written,

146
00:04:20.960 --> 00:04:22.360
and the rule was wrong.

147
00:04:22.360 --> 00:04:24.280
Complaints happen in one percent of cases

148
00:04:24.280 --> 00:04:25.500
or fewer.

149
00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:27.780
With three hundred on each side,

150
00:04:27.780 --> 00:04:30.180
six versus one is noise.

151
00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:33.140
Tolerance is about two standard errors of

152
00:04:33.140 --> 00:04:34.140
the baseline rate,

153
00:04:34.140 --> 00:04:36.300
which here comes to zero point zero

154
00:04:36.300 --> 00:04:36.800
one.

155
00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:37.980
Renata updated the worksheet,

156
00:04:37.980 --> 00:04:39.980
and the idea went into memory as

157
00:04:39.980 --> 00:04:41.800
discarded, with its numbers.

158
00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:42.920
In cycle three,

159
00:04:42.920 --> 00:04:44.760
the idea waiting in the queue came

160
00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:45.960
back:

161
00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:48.460
open by mentioning a specific point from

162
00:04:48.460 --> 00:04:49.800
the client’s review.

163
00:04:49.800 --> 00:04:53.095
The Experimenter recalculated using the real number:

164
00:04:53.095 --> 00:04:55.440
two hundred twelve proposals per version,

165
00:04:55.440 --> 00:04:56.540
about nine weeks.

166
00:04:56.540 --> 00:04:57.620
A new idea,

167
00:04:57.620 --> 00:04:59.980
to mention the price right after the

168
00:04:59.980 --> 00:05:02.500
greeting, was vetoed because it was likely

169
00:05:02.500 --> 00:05:04.120
to increase opt-outs.

170
00:05:04.120 --> 00:05:06.980
Cycle four: three hundred on each side.

171
00:05:06.980 --> 00:05:09.552
Replies went from nineteen point three to

172
00:05:09.552 --> 00:05:10.400
twenty-nine point three percent,

173
00:05:10.400 --> 00:05:12.680
with a chance of luck of four

174
00:05:12.680 --> 00:05:14.480
in a thousand.

175
00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:16.300
Complaints were four versus two,

176
00:05:16.300 --> 00:05:17.880
within the new tolerance.

177
00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:18.900
Verdict:

178
00:05:18.900 --> 00:05:19.820
B won.

179
00:05:19.820 --> 00:05:21.420
The card came in at a cost

180
00:05:21.420 --> 00:05:22.920
of about forty-five reais.

181
00:05:22.920 --> 00:05:25.220
Renata approved it.

182
00:05:25.220 --> 00:05:27.280
Version two became official,

183
00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:29.600
while version one stayed in the history,

184
00:05:29.600 --> 00:05:31.220
with a rollback button.

185
00:05:31.220 --> 00:05:33.600
Now, one detail worth remembering.

186
00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:36.040
Conversion, the number Renata actually cared about,

187
00:05:36.040 --> 00:05:38.620
went from three to three point seven

188
00:05:38.620 --> 00:05:39.120
percent.

189
00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:41.380
And with this sample size,

190
00:05:41.380 --> 00:05:42.740
that proves nothing.

191
00:05:42.740 --> 00:05:44.900
The loop proved that more people reply,

192
00:05:44.900 --> 00:05:46.300
not that more people buy.

193
00:05:46.300 --> 00:05:48.080
The meta-agent can see this.

194
00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:49.260
It doesn’t look at the client;

195
00:05:49.260 --> 00:05:50.200
it looks at the loop.

196
00:05:50.200 --> 00:05:51.260
Cost:

197
00:05:51.260 --> 00:05:53.220
cycle four used ninety percent of the

198
00:05:53.220 --> 00:05:55.145
limit, an alert.

199
00:05:55.145 --> 00:05:57.147
Vetoes: two of four ideas,

200
00:05:57.147 --> 00:05:58.000
an alert.

201
00:05:58.000 --> 00:05:59.900
Cadence: promised weekly,

202
00:05:59.900 --> 00:06:01.487
but the actual gaps were twelve,

203
00:06:01.487 --> 00:06:02.520
one, and twelve weeks.

204
00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:03.620
And the main finding:

205
00:06:03.620 --> 00:06:04.620
two

206
00:06:04.620 --> 00:06:06.240
ideas raised

207
00:06:06.240 --> 00:06:07.840
replies by ten points,

208
00:06:07.840 --> 00:06:09.740
and neither moved conversion.

209
00:06:09.740 --> 00:06:12.200
The meta-agent fixes nothing.

210
00:06:12.200 --> 00:06:12.980
It shows you,

211
00:06:12.980 --> 00:06:14.380
and you decide.

212
00:06:14.380 --> 00:06:15.700
Time for practice.

213
00:06:15.700 --> 00:06:17.780
Pause the video and answer in writing;

214
00:06:17.780 --> 00:06:20.040
give yourself about ten minutes.

215
00:06:20.040 --> 00:06:22.720
Renata opens the cycle four report.

216
00:06:22.800 --> 00:06:25.380
She has fifty reais and three ideas

217
00:06:25.380 --> 00:06:26.060
in the queue:

218
00:06:26.060 --> 00:06:27.220
a new greeting,

219
00:06:27.220 --> 00:06:28.860
sending the proposal as audio,

220
00:06:28.860 --> 00:06:31.540
and a meta-agent suggestion.

221
00:06:31.540 --> 00:06:34.340
Question one: why was the eighty-word idea

222
00:06:34.340 --> 00:06:36.920
discarded, and what changed in the worksheet?

223
00:06:36.920 --> 00:06:39.540
Question two: what three conditions allowed the

224
00:06:39.540 --> 00:06:40.640
promotion?

225
00:06:40.640 --> 00:06:44.260
Question three: what should cycle five test?

226
00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:46.460
Compare your answers with the key.

227
00:06:46.460 --> 00:06:48.300
I’ll give away the third:

228
00:06:48.300 --> 00:06:50.320
neither the greeting nor audio.

229
00:06:50.320 --> 00:06:52.480
Cycle five should replace the signal metric

230
00:06:52.480 --> 00:06:54.540
with something closer to closing a sale,

231
00:06:54.540 --> 00:06:56.860
such as a requested appointment or the

232
00:06:56.860 --> 00:06:57.360
amount.

233
00:06:57.080 --> 00:06:58.240
The

234
00:06:58.240 --> 00:06:59.340
signal rose twice,

235
00:06:59.340 --> 00:07:01.060
and the target didn’t move.

236
00:07:01.060 --> 00:07:01.700
Lesson two.

237
00:07:01.700 --> 00:07:03.720
Support is where a company hears the

238
00:07:03.720 --> 00:07:06.060
customer most and learns the least.

239
00:07:06.060 --> 00:07:09.600
Each ticket gets closed and forgotten.

240
00:07:09.720 --> 00:07:13.260
Meet Marcos, support manager for software used

241
00:07:13.260 --> 00:07:14.640
by small shops.

242
00:07:14.640 --> 00:07:16.940
Eight people handle about eight hundred tickets

243
00:07:16.940 --> 00:07:17.520
a month.

244
00:07:17.520 --> 00:07:20.720
Each closed ticket leaves a trace:

245
00:07:20.940 --> 00:07:22.540
issue, time,

246
00:07:22.540 --> 00:07:24.160
whether it was resolved,

247
00:07:24.160 --> 00:07:25.580
whether it came back,

248
00:07:25.580 --> 00:07:27.200
and the rating.

249
00:07:27.200 --> 00:07:29.120
That’s a ready-made spreadsheet.

250
00:07:29.120 --> 00:07:31.080
The thing no one does is read

251
00:07:31.080 --> 00:07:31.580
it.

252
00:07:31.420 --> 00:07:33.640
The Observer only counts and groups.

253
00:07:33.640 --> 00:07:34.560
The first time,

254
00:07:34.560 --> 00:07:36.460
a number Marcos knew but had never

255
00:07:36.460 --> 00:07:37.913
seen written down appeared:

256
00:07:37.913 --> 00:07:40.720
thirty-seven percent of tickets were about exporting

257
00:07:40.720 --> 00:07:41.420
a report.

258
00:07:41.420 --> 00:07:43.420
The clinic owner has the same pile,

259
00:07:43.420 --> 00:07:46.400
just smaller: forty messages a month asking

260
00:07:46.400 --> 00:07:47.380
about the price.

261
00:07:47.380 --> 00:07:49.080
She’s never counted them either.

262
00:07:49.080 --> 00:07:50.760
A support loop has the same shape

263
00:07:50.760 --> 00:07:51.960
as a sales loop:

264
00:07:51.960 --> 00:07:54.920
maximize one thing without making others worse.

265
00:07:54.920 --> 00:07:57.080
Marcos wrote: maximize

266
00:07:57.080 --> 00:07:58.950
first-contact resolution, from

267
00:07:58.950 --> 00:08:00.060
fifty-five to sixty-five percent,

268
00:08:00.060 --> 00:08:02.360
without worsening satisfaction,

269
00:08:02.360 --> 00:08:03.760
seven-day reopen rate,

270
00:08:03.760 --> 00:08:05.440
or customer time.

271
00:08:05.440 --> 00:08:07.600
The earlier version was dangerous:

272
00:08:07.600 --> 00:08:10.220
reduce average time from twelve to eight

273
00:08:10.220 --> 00:08:10.800
minutes.

274
00:08:10.800 --> 00:08:13.020
Pause and answer the lesson’s test:

275
00:08:13.020 --> 00:08:14.960
what would a loop learn from that

276
00:08:14.960 --> 00:08:16.160
goal,

277
00:08:16.420 --> 00:08:19.340
with no “without worsening” clause?

278
00:08:19.340 --> 00:08:21.120
It would learn to close tickets without

279
00:08:21.120 --> 00:08:22.000
solving the problem.

280
00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:24.240
The customer reopens the ticket,

281
00:08:24.240 --> 00:08:26.100
and the time still looks good.

282
00:08:26.100 --> 00:08:27.340
The good news:

283
00:08:27.340 --> 00:08:29.140
rates here are high,

284
00:08:29.140 --> 00:08:31.260
and proving fifty against

285
00:08:31.260 --> 00:08:33.960
sixty-five takes about one hundred seventy tickets

286
00:08:33.960 --> 00:08:34.720
per version.

287
00:08:34.720 --> 00:08:37.120
The clinic would need fifteen hundred.

288
00:08:37.120 --> 00:08:38.920
That’s where the loop turns faster.

289
00:08:39.200 --> 00:08:40.900
With thirty-seven percent in hand,

290
00:08:40.900 --> 00:08:43.300
the loop runs like the sales loop.

291
00:08:43.300 --> 00:08:45.887
The Critic writes a strong critique because

292
00:08:45.887 --> 00:08:47.820
